# crswd configuration — EXAMPLE
#
# Copy this to ~/.config/crswd/config and uncomment only what you mean to
# change. Every setting below is commented out, so a copy with nothing
# uncommented is a daemon that behaves exactly as one with no configuration
# file at all — which is every crswd deployed before this file existed. The
# value shown on each line is the default where there is one that can be
# written down, and an illustration where there is not; either way the block
# above it names the default in words.
#
# The daemon reads this once, before it binds or spawns anything. Nothing here
# can be changed while it runs, and a value that would weaken a bound is a
# startup failure rather than a warning: sessions run with
# --dangerously-skip-permissions, and these settings are what stands in for the
# permission prompt that is gone.
#
#
# WHERE THIS FILE GOES
#
#   $CRSW_CONFIG_FILE, if it is set — it names the file outright
#   else $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/crswd/config
#   else ~/.config/crswd/config
#
# CRSW_CONFIG_FILE is taken exactly as written, a relative path included. In a
# systemd unit a relative path resolves against that unit's WorkingDirectory
# and not against the directory you ran systemctl from, so name an absolute one
# unless you mean that.
#
# The file is not required and its absence is never an error. A file that is
# there and cannot be opened is one: an unreadable file is a file you expect to
# be read, and starting on the environment instead would ignore every bound you
# wrote in it.
#
# `crswd config check` reads it and says what it makes, without starting
# anything. If it stops loading, the daemon starts from the config.bak beside
# it instead — loudly, naming both files, because it is then running on the
# older one and your most recent edits are not in effect.
#
#
# THE FORMAT, AND WHY IT IS THIS ONE
#
#   key = value
#   # a comment
#
# `#` is a comment marker at the start of a line and nowhere else. There are no
# trailing comments: a shared secret may legitimately contain a `#`, and
# stripping from the first one would truncate that secret into a daemon that
# starts, looks healthy, and rejects every request it is sent.
#
# The separator is the FIRST `=`. start_commands always carries an `=` inside
# its value, and a parser that refused that ambiguity would be refusing valid
# configuration.
#
# A key is its environment variable without the CRSW_ prefix, lower-cased:
# allowed_roots is CRSW_ALLOWED_ROOTS. That is a rule rather than a table, so
# every variable named in .env.example is a key here, and the reverse. Keys are
# lower-case [a-z0-9_]; a key outside that alphabet is refused rather than
# folded, so each setting has exactly one spelling.
#
# The environment still wins. Precedence is flag, then environment, then this
# file, then the built-in default — so a container or a unit can override one
# value without writing a file, and a stale file on a host cannot silently
# override the environment a deployment was configured with. The /settings page
# names which layer each value actually came from, which is the question worth
# answering when an edit here appears to have done nothing.
#
# A misspelled key is refused, never skipped, and so is a key set twice. An
# `alowed_roots` that quietly did nothing is how a containment boundary ends up
# unset on a daemon whose operator believes they set it.
#
# It is not JSON, and that is the decision this format exists for: JSON has no
# comments, so everything you are reading would have been deleted from it, and
# what each bound is for is the most useful thing this file has to say. YAML
# and TOML were never candidates — neither has a standard-library parser and
# neither is safe to hand-roll, and a dependency for one would create the
# go.sum this repository does not have (docs/security.md §5).
#
# NOTHING IN THE DAEMON WRITES THIS FILE. It is yours, and a daemon that
# reformatted it would have taken a decision that was not its to take.
# `crswd config migrate` is the only code in this repository that writes a
# configuration file at all.

# The schema this file was written against. Absent means 1, which is what every
# hand-written file is. A number higher than the daemon understands is refused
# rather than read optimistically: the reason to raise it is that a key changed
# meaning, and guessing at the new meaning is how a containment boundary ends
# up set to something you did not write.
version = 1


# --- Required. There is no default, and no way to start without it. ---

# The HMAC shared secret the API client signs with. At least 32 bytes; unset or
# shorter is a startup failure, because an absent secret is an absent
# authentication layer and a daemon that starts with one is worse than a daemon
# that does not start. It is never logged, never returned in an error, and
# never echoed back — not even its length.
#
# Generate one with `openssl rand -hex 32`.
#
# A file that sets this, or access_allowed_emails, must be mode 0600: the
# daemon refuses to start from one that any other account on this host can read
# or write. That refusal fires only on a file that actually holds one of the
# two — refusing over the mode of a file holding nothing but allowed_roots
# would be demanding a change that protects nothing.
# shared_secret = paste the output of openssl rand -hex 32 here


# --- The blast radius. Read docs/security.md before widening anything here. ---

# Colon-separated (`:`) absolute paths. A session may only run in a directory
# contained by one of these, and this is the real containment control — keep it
# narrow. Every entry must already exist as a directory that resolves; one that
# does not is a startup failure, as is an empty entry. Two spellings of the
# same directory collapse to one.
#
# Default: $HOME/code, announced by a banner on stderr at every start. $HOME
# itself is never the default: SSH keys, cloud credentials and browser profiles
# all live directly under it, which would make the allowlist decorative.
# allowed_roots = /home/you/code:/home/you/work

# Offer the immediate subdirectories of each approved root as
# working-directory suggestions on the create form. Off by default, and that
# default is the whole point: listing a filesystem is a disclosure, however
# mild, and you opt into it rather than finding your directory names on a page
# you never asked to have read the host.
#
# It widens nothing. allowed_roots is still the control — a suggestion is
# checked against the roots as it is offered, and every path, picked or typed,
# is resolved and checked again on the create it arrives with.
#
# true or false. Anything else — `yes`, `on`, `2` — is a startup failure rather
# than a silent off: you meant on, and a daemon that read it as off would run
# without the thing you asked for and say nothing.
# discover_roots = false

# Directories to offer on the create form on top of the approved roots, which
# are always offered, and any discovered children. Comma-separated (`,`), so a
# directory whose name contains a comma is one you type instead of pick.
#
# Every entry must be absolute. A relative one is a startup failure because no
# create could ever accept it, and an empty one — a stray comma — is a startup
# failure rather than a blank line in the picker. Nothing here is checked
# against the filesystem: these are paths you typed, and offering one says
# nothing about what is there, not even that it exists.
#
# A path outside allowed_roots may be listed, and it is refused on create
# exactly as a typed one is — same response, same audit record. That is the
# contract working rather than a hole: this list is a convenience and
# allowed_roots is the control.
#
# Default: empty.
# workdir_suggestions = /srv/scratch,/srv/second

# host:port for the daemon's own listener. Loopback only, and the host must be
# an IP literal: a name can be pointed anywhere by /etc/hosts or a resolver,
# which would move the bind off loopback without changing this value.
# Reachability comes from the Cloudflare Tunnel and never from this.
#
# Default: 127.0.0.1:8765.
# listen = 127.0.0.1:8765

# How many sessions may exist at once. Past it the daemon refuses to create
# rather than degrading the host it is running on. At least 1; anything that is
# not a whole number is a startup failure.
#
# Default: 5.
# max_sessions = 5

# Tear every session down when the daemon stops, which is what it did before
# sessions survived a restart. Off by default: a graceful restart is
# overwhelmingly the common case, destroying a fleet to redeploy a binary is a
# cost nobody asked for, and startup adoption reclaims what a restart left.
#
# This build does not read the key. It parses, and /settings renders it, but
# the loader has no case for it yet — so sessions survive a clean stop whatever
# is written here.
# destroy_on_shutdown = false

# How long a session may live from creation, whatever it is doing. Go duration
# syntax: `24h`, `90m`. It must be positive. There is deliberately no way to
# say "never": a session that can outlive the daemon's own memory of why it
# exists is what the bound is written against.
#
# Default: 24h.
# session_lifetime = 24h

# The ceiling a per-session lifetime override may not exceed. A create asking
# for more is refused rather than quietly clamped — a caller who asked for
# thirty days and was given one believes they have thirty, and nothing tells
# them otherwise until the session is gone. Below session_lifetime it is a
# startup failure, because every create would then be refused by a ceiling
# under its own default.
#
# Default: whatever session_lifetime is.
# session_lifetime_max = 24h

# How long a session may sit without a request before the reaper takes it,
# measured from its last activity and moving with it. It may not be negative,
# and longer than session_lifetime is a startup failure because it could then
# never fire — a setting that does nothing is worse than one that refuses,
# since nothing tells you it did nothing.
#
# `0` is not a disable here: zero reads as unset further down and the built-in
# 60m applies. Switching idle reaping off belongs to one session, as a
# create-time override, rather than to every session this daemon will start.
#
# Default: 60m.
# idle_timeout = 60m

# The ceiling for a per-session idle override, as session_lifetime_max is for
# lifetimes. Below idle_timeout is a startup failure.
#
# Default: whatever idle_timeout is.
# idle_timeout_max = 60m

# How many sessions may be created per minute before the daemon starts refusing
# creates. At least 1.
#
# Default: 6.
# create_rate_per_min = 6

# The largest request body the daemon will read, in bytes. Past it a request is
# refused rather than truncated. At least 1.
#
# Default: 65536.
# max_body_bytes = 65536


# --- Layer 1: the browser door. All three of these, or none of them. ---
#
# None of the three means this daemon serves the API and admits nobody to the
# dashboard, which is a deployment it supports and says loudly at every start.
# Some of them is a startup failure: a team domain without an audience is a
# half-configured door, and a daemon that started anyway would refuse every
# login while looking correctly configured.

# Your Cloudflare Access team domain, normally <team>.cloudflareaccess.com. It
# is one value because two things must agree: the expected issuer and the key
# set are both derived from it, and configuring them separately would allow an
# issuer and a key set that do not belong to each other — a validator checking
# signatures against the wrong authority.
#
# A full origin is accepted too. http:// is refused unless the host is a
# loopback IP literal; that carve-out exists so the acceptance tests can serve
# a key set they control without a Cloudflare account, and it skips no
# validation.
#
# Default: none, and the dashboard admits nobody.
# access_team_domain = your-team.cloudflareaccess.com

# The Access application's AUD tag, from the application's settings in the
# Cloudflare dashboard. It is compared for equality against the assertion's
# audience and never parsed, so an assertion minted for another application on
# the same team — or for another team entirely — is refused.
#
# Default: none.
# access_aud = the AUD tag from your Access application

# Comma-separated addresses allowed to reach the dashboard. The edge is the
# gate; this is the daemon asserting the gate is configured as you believe, so
# an Access policy somebody widened in the dashboard does not widen this. An
# entry that is empty, or that carries a space, is a startup failure —
# whitespace inside an address is the separator typed wrong, and left to run it
# is an address that never matches with nothing saying why.
#
# It is treated as a secret. It authenticates nobody, but it names *who* may
# reach a daemon that runs unsandboxed code on this host, which is worth
# exactly as little publication as the secret that authenticates them: a file
# setting it must be mode 0600, and /settings reports it as present or absent
# and never spells it.
#
# Default: none.
# access_allowed_emails = you@example.com,colleague@example.com


# --- Bounds on what the dashboard may hold open and believe. ---

# How many live output streams may be open at once — the one thing a browser
# can hold open indefinitely. The default is twice max_sessions, so every
# session on a fully loaded host is watchable from two tabs before the daemon
# starts refusing. At least 1.
#
# Default: 10.
# max_streams = 10

# The largest screen a pane capture may return, in lines. A capture past it is
# refused rather than shortened: half a screen is a wrong screen, not a smaller
# one. At least 1.
#
# 200 is far above the 24 lines a session this daemon starts actually has —
# nothing ever attaches to one, so it keeps tmux's 80x24 default. What the
# bound is watching for is a capture that reaches into the scrollback, where
# tmux's own history-limit default of 2000 lines is an order of magnitude past
# this. It should fire when the assumption behind it has broken, never because
# a pane is merely full.
#
# Default: 200.
# pane_bound = 200


# --- What the daemon types into a new session's shell. ---
#
# A create carries a NAME from the set below and never a command line. A route
# that took a command line would make this a general remote-shell service
# behind whatever authenticates it, rather than a Claude session manager — the
# same reason a working directory is chosen from allowed_roots instead of being
# named freely.
#
# Every command line here is checked at startup rather than at delivery, so you
# learn about a bad one from a daemon that refuses to boot instead of from a
# session that came up three quarters configured. A `;` is refused, because
# tmux's parser eats one before the line is ever typed; so is any control
# character, which would submit the line early. A command line may not contain
# a comma, which is the list separator. `{name}` is the one substitution the
# daemon makes — the session's own name, restricted to [a-zA-Z0-9-] so it
# cannot change the shape of the line — and any other `{token}` is refused
# rather than typed at a shell.

# The command line bound to the name `default`, which is what a create that
# asks for nothing in particular runs.
#
# Default: claude --dangerously-skip-permissions, byte for byte what every
# session started before this was configurable at all.
# start_command = claude --dangerously-skip-permissions

# The named set a create may choose from, spelled as name/command pairs
# separated by commas. Note the `=` inside the value: the parser splits on the
# first one only, which is why this line means what it looks like. Names are
# [a-z0-9-] and at most 32 characters, because a name reaches the audit trail.
#
# Naming `default` here as well as in start_command is a startup failure rather
# than one of them silently winning. Which of two spellings takes effect is the
# question you can least afford to guess about: the answer decides what gets
# typed into an unsandboxed shell on every create that names no command.
#
# Default: none, so the set is exactly the one `default` above.
# start_commands = rc=claude remote-control --name {name}

# Which entry of that set the dashboard's remote-control switch means. It
# carries a name for the same reason a create does: a switch wired to a name is
# one you can read out of your own configuration and change here, and a switch
# wired to a command line would be the thing the allowlist exists to prevent.
#
# Unset means `rc` when you have configured a command by that name, and no
# switch at all when you have not — you are never offered a control whose only
# outcome is a refusal. Set to a name the set does not have, it is a startup
# failure: you have said which command you mean, and a switch that quietly
# started plain sessions instead would be worse than no switch.
#
# Default: rc, when a command by that name exists.
# remote_control_command = rc
